    "https://www.jobs.cmich.edu/postings/45235": {
      "title": "DIRECTOR / RESIDENTIAL EDUCATION",
      "description": "CMU Applicant Portal | DIRECTOR / RESIDENTIAL EDUCATION Skip to Main Content Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Administrative Office Assistant Custodial / Landscape / Skilled Trades Dispatcher Faculty Lieutenant Police Post-Doctoral Research Professional & Admin-Hourly Professional & Admin-Salary Public Broadcasting-Hourly Public Broadcasting-Salary Senior Officer Sergeant Supervisory Technical Temporary Staff For Career Opportunities with CMU Medical Education Partners (CMEP), please read the following: CMU Medical Education Partners (CMEP) is a joint partner with Central Michigan University (CMU), Covenant Healthcare, and St. Mary’s of Michigan. CMEP is located in Saginaw, Michigan, and is a separate nonprofit legal entity from CMU. The faculty of CMEP works collaboratively and interactively with the College of Medicine faculty at CMU to deliver an innovative curriculum to CMU medical students. Because CMU Medical Education Partners (CMEP) is a separate legal entity (and employer) from CMU, the employment opportunities available through CMEP are not employment positions with CMU. When you click the link below, you will be leaving the CMU Career Opportunities webpage. CMU is not responsible for the content of the webpage to which you are being redirected. After reading the above information, please click here if you are interested in employment opportunities with CMU Medical Education Partners. DIRECTOR / RESIDENTIAL EDUCATION Below you will find the details for the position including any supplementary documentation and questions you should review before applying for the opening.  To apply for the position, please click the Apply for this Job link/button. If you would like to bookmark this position for later review, click on the Bookmark this Job link. If you would like to print a copy of this position for your records, click on the Print Preview link. If you need to finish applying, click on the Continue this Job Application button. Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Central Michigan University is dedicated to fostering an environment that is reflective of the communities we serve. We are especially interested in highly qualified candidates who will advance and promote CMU’s mission, vision, and leadership standards. You must submit an on-line application in order to be considered as an applicant for this position. Cover letters may be addressed to the Hiring Committee. This position will remain open until filled. The university reserves the right to close the recruitment process once a sufficient applicant pool has been identified. For best consideration, please submit application materials by September 4, 2026 . Position Information Position Information Posting Number S-3939 Position Number 33970 Type of Recruitment External Position Title DIRECTOR / RESIDENTIAL EDUCATION Position Summary Reporting to the Executive Director of Residence Life and Chief Housing Officer, the Director of Residential Education provides strategic, administrative, and organizational leadership for the residential education program serving students across the University’s residence halls and apartment communities. The Director is responsible for establishing and advancing a comprehensive residential education strategy that promotes student belonging, learning, well-being, engagement, leadership development, and persistence. The position translates institutional and divisional priorities into coherent residential practices, staffing models, learning experiences, response systems, and performance expectations. As a senior leader within Residence Life, the Director moves the Residential Education unit beyond a primarily reactive and relationship-dependent operating model toward one characterized by clear functional ownership, consistent practices, defined decision rights, measurable outcomes, sustainable workloads, and intentional collaboration. The Director evaluates how work is structured and distributed across the unit and implements organizational, procedural, and resource changes necessary to strengthen effectiveness, consistency, accountability, and staff capacity. The Director provides leadership for professional and paraprofessional staffing; residential learning and engagement; student leadership development; community standards; crisis and incident response; student care and referral practices; professional development; staff recruitment and retention; and the assessment of residential student outcomes. The Director serves as a member of the Residence Life leadership team and works in close partnership with leaders responsible for facilities, housing assignments and operations, student care, assessment, and other departmental functions. The Director also establishes and manages strategic partnerships with campus units whose systems, services, and decisions materially affect the residential student experience. This is a live-out position. The Director provides senior-level consultation and escalation support for significant residential incidents and emergencies but does not routinely participate in the primary on-call rotation. Required Qualifications Bachelor’s degree in student affairs, higher education, or related field. At least five (5) years of progressively responsible experience in residence life, residential education, housing, student affairs, or a closely related area. Significant experience supervising, developing, and evaluating full-time professional staff, including employees who supervise others. Demonstrated experience providing strategic leadership for a complex residential education or student affairs operation. Demonstrated success leading organizational change, implementing new systems or practices, and building support across multiple stakeholder groups. Experience developing and implementing residential learning, student engagement, community development, or student leadership initiatives. Experience with student conduct, crisis response, student care, behavioral intervention, or emergency management in a college or university environment. Experience using assessment, operational data, student feedback, and institutional priorities to evaluate programs and guide decision-making. Demonstrated ability to evaluate organizational structure, workload, functional ownership, and resource allocation and to implement changes that strengthen effectiveness and sustainability. Demonstrated ability to establish clear standards, procedures, decision rights, and accountability mechanisms across a decentralized or multifaceted organization. Demonstrated ability to develop and manage productive partnerships with academic, student support, public safety, facilities, conduct, counseling, and other institutional stakeholders. Demonstrated multicultural competence and the ability to lead inclusive residential communities serving students with varied identities, experiences, and needs. Advanced knowledge of student development, student learning, organizational development, and contemporary residential education practices. Demonstrated organizational, analytical, administrative, and problem-solving ability. Demonstrated ability to communicate effectively with students, families, employees, senior administrators, and campus partners, both orally and in writing. Ability to exercise sound judgment in complex, sensitive, politically nuanced, and high-impact situations. Proficiency with contemporary administrative technology, data systems, case-management platforms, and standard office applications. Ability to work evenings and weekends and respond to significant incidents outside regular business hours when necessary. Preferred Qualifications Master’s degree in student affairs, higher education, or related field. Seven (7) or more years of progressively responsible experience in residence life, residential education, housing, student affairs, or a closely related area. Experience providing leadership within a residential education program of comparable size, scope, and complexity. Experience supervising multiple layers of professional staff. Demonstrated success redesigning organizational structures, staffing models, supervisory systems, or operational practices. Experience implementing a residential curriculum or other intentional, learning-centered residential model. Experience assessing the relationship between residential initiatives and student belonging, engagement, retention, academic progress, or well-being. Experience establishing cross-functional service agreements, escalation protocols, partnership frameworks, or shared accountability structures. Experience managing budgets and reallocating resources in response to changing priorities and demonstrated outcomes. Experience with Advocate, Maxient, StarRez, or comparable housing, conduct, case-management, or assessment systems. Active engagement in relevant professional associations and continuing professional development. Duties & Responsibilities Strategic Leadership and Organizational Direction: Establishes and advances a comprehensive vision and strategic direction for Residential Education that aligns with the missions, priorities, and student-success objectives of Residence Life, University Engagement and Student Affairs, and Central Michigan University. Translates strategic priorities into clear operating expectations, implementation plans, performance measures, staffing responsibilities, timelines, and accountability structures. Leads the implementation, evaluation, and continuous refinement of the Residential Education strategic plan. Evaluates emerging student needs, professional standards, institutional trends, legal and regulatory expectations, and peer practices to ensure that the residential education program remains responsive, contemporary, and effective. Advises the Executive Director on matters involving residential education strategy, organizational structure, staffing, student experience, risk, resource allocation, and institutional policy. Represents Residential Education in departmental, divisional, University, and external settings and articulates the strategic value of the residential experience to senior leaders and other stakeholders. Organizational Effectiveness and Sustainable Work Design: Evaluates the distribution of work across Residential Education to ensure that staffing structures, supervisory relationships, functional assignments, and employee workloads support institutional priorities and sustainable performance. Clarifies ownership for core functions and ensures that work is assigned at the appropriate organizational level. Establishes decision-rights frameworks that distinguish matters appropriate for paraprofessional staff, Residence Hall Directors, Assistant Directors, the Director, and the Executive Director. Identifies duplicative, low-value, unclear, or inconsistently assigned responsibilities and leads efforts to eliminate, consolidate, automate, centralize, or redistribute work. Develops systems that reduce unnecessary administrative burden and allow professional staff to devote greater capacity to student engagement, supervision, proactive intervention, assessment, and strategic work. Regularly assesses spans of control, role clarity, staff capacity, and organizational performance and recommends structural or resource changes when needed. Creates mechanisms for employees to surface operational barriers, recurring challenges, workload concerns, and opportunities for improvement. Standards, Systems, and Operational Consistency: Establishes and maintains a coherent framework of foundational Residential Education practices, including written procedures, service standards, documentation expectations, escalation pathways, response protocols, and quality-assurance mechanisms. Ensures that students receive a reasonably consistent baseline experience across residential communities while preserving appropriate flexibility for community context, innovation, and professional judgment. Leads the development and periodic review of policies and procedures related to community management, student engagement, supervision, conduct, care, crisis response, desk operations, opening and closing, break housing, transitions, and other Residential Education functions. Ensures that procedures are accessible, understood, regularly reinforced, and supported through appropriate training and reference materials. Establishes systems to monitor implementation fidelity, identify variations in practice, address performance gaps, and disseminate effective approaches across the department. Uses recurring incidents, service failures, student concerns, and staff feedback to identify systemic weaknesses. Residential Learning, Engagement, and Student Success: Provides strategic leadership for a comprehensive residential learning and engagement model designed to strengthen student belonging, interpersonal connection, personal development, academic engagement, well-being, leadership capacity, and persistence. Ensures that residential learning initiatives are grounded in clearly defined outcomes and informed by student-development theory, institutional priorities, assessment findings, and the needs of the residential population. Guides the implementation and continued development of the residential curriculum and related community-development strategies. Moves the department from an activity- and volume-oriented model toward an outcomes-oriented approach that prioritizes the quality, relevance, accessibility, and demonstrated impact of residential interventions. Ensures that engagement strategies include intentional outreach to students who may be disconnected, under-engaged, experiencing transition challenges, or at increased risk of attrition. Develops partnerships that embed academic support, student-success resources, well-being initiatives, and other institutional services within or in close connection to residential communities. Promotes meaningful student voice, self-governance, peer leadership, and participation in decisions affecting the residential experience. Assessment, Data, and Continuous Improvement: Establishes a comprehensive assessment strategy for Residential Education and ensures that programmatic and operational decisions are informed by credible evidence. Defines and monitors key performance indicators related to belonging, engagement, learning, staff capacity, response quality, consistency of practice, student satisfaction, retention, and other departmental outcomes. Uses quantitative and qualitative data to determine which programs, practices, staffing investments, and interventions produce meaningful student outcomes. Ensures that data are used to improve practice and allocate resources rather than collected solely for reporting purposes. Produces regular analyses and reports that communicate Residential Education outcomes, emerging trends, areas of risk, resource implications, and recommendations to departmental and divisional leadership. Creates structured cycles of implementation, measurement, reflection, and adjustment throughout the Residential Education organization. Collaborates with assessment, institutional research, enrollment, academic, and student-success partners to evaluate the relationship between the residential experience and broader institutional outcomes. Staff Leadership, Supervision, and Professional Development: Directly supervises, coaches, evaluates, and develops the Assistant Directors of Residence Life and ensures that they exercise appropriate director-level delegated authority within their assigned portfolios. Establishes a coherent supervisory philosophy and baseline expectations for supervision throughout Residential Education. Ensures that supervisors provide employees with clear expectations, regular feedback, developmental coaching, appropriate accountability, and meaningful opportunities for professional growth. Develops succession capacity and prepares employees to assume broader responsibilities and exercise increasingly complex professional judgment. Provides strategic oversight for the recruitment, selection, onboarding, retention, performance management, and professional development of Residential Education staff. Ensures that professional-development investments are differentiated by role and competency and are connected to identified organizational needs. Builds a learning organization in which employees examine outcomes, share effective practices, acknowledge mistakes, engage in constructive disagreement, and adapt their work based on evidence. Promotes an organizational culture that combines strong relational trust with clear structures, consistent expectations, accountability, and shared responsibility. Training and Organizational Preparedness: Establishes a comprehensive, competency-based training and development strategy for professional and paraprofessional staff. Ensures that training is practical, role-specific, sequenced appropriately, grounded in adult-learning principles, and connected to the situations employees are expected to manage. Oversees the development and maintenance of accessible response guides, decision trees, protocols, and other tools that support consistent action. Evaluates training effectiveness using participant feedback, competency measures, incident reviews, supervisory observations, and operational outcomes. Ensures that lessons from incidents, after-action reviews, assessment findings, and changing student needs are incorporated into future training. Student Care, Conduct, Crisis Response, and Risk Management: Provides strategic oversight for Residential Education’s role in student care, community standards, conduct response, behavioral intervention, and crisis management. Establishes clear boundaries between Residential Education responsibilities and the responsibilities of CMU CARES , Student Conduct, Counseling, Police, Facilities Management, and other campus units. Ensures that incident and care reports are accurate, timely, appropriately routed, and reviewed for patterns, quality, consistency, and risk. Creates and maintains clear escalation pathways for significant student, community, safety, facility, and personnel concerns. Provides senior-level consultation and decision support during complex, high-risk, or high-impact incidents. Ensures that Residential Education’s response practices are educational, equitable, trauma-informed, developmentally appropriate, legally compliant, and aligned with University policy. Leads or participates in after-action reviews and ensures that identified improvements are translated into changes in training, procedure, staffing, communication, or partnership expectations. Collaborates with appropriate University officials regarding significant student concerns, institutional risk, parent or family communication, media-sensitive matters, and emergency response. Strategic Partnership Management: Moves Residential Education from informal relationship management toward an intentional partnership-management model with campus stakeholders whose work directly affects residential students. Identifies and maintains the department’s portfolio of critical partnerships, including relationships with academic affairs, student success, CMU CARES , counseling, student conduct, public safety, facilities, accessibility services, enrollment management, and other relevant areas. Defines liaison responsibilities and ensures that employees representing Residence Life in cross-functional relationships have clear authority, expectations, communication responsibilities, and escalation pathways. Establishes shared service expectations, operating agreements, response standards, meeting structures, and issue-resolution processes with key campus partners when appropriate. Develops mechanisms for identifying, tracking, escalating, and resolving recurring cross-functional problems. Uses partnership data, student feedback, response timelines, recurring issue analysis, and stakeholder feedback to evaluate the effectiveness of institutional coordination. Advocates for changes to external systems and practices that create unnecessary barriers, delays, or frustration for residential students and employees. Recruitment, Selection, and Talent Management: Provides strategic oversight for the recruitment and selection of professional, graduate, paraprofessional, and student staff within Residential Education. Evaluates recruitment and selection practices to ensure they produce capable, diverse, student-centered, and mission-aligned employee teams. Develops long-term talent pipelines and recruitment strategies responsive to labor-market conditions and institutional staffing needs. Ensures that selection processes assess the competencies, judgment, adaptability, and leadership capacity required for success in increasingly complex residential environments. Represents Central Michigan University and Residence Life at appropriate professional recruitment events and develops relationships that strengthen the department’s professional reputation and applicant pool. Reviews recruitment, retention, vacancy, performance, and employee-experience data and recommends changes to compensation, workload, structure, supervision, professional development, or workplace practices. Resource Stewardship and Administrative Leadership Develops and manages budgets, staffing allocations, and other resources assigned to Residential Education. Aligns resource allocation with strategic priorities, demonstrated student needs, assessed outcomes, institutional risk, and organizational capacity. Evaluates the effectiveness and return on investment of major programs, staffing models, training efforts, and operational practices. Identifies the resource implications of new initiatives and clearly communicates associated tradeoffs, opportunity costs, and implementation requirements. Ensures compliance with University policies, applicable laws and regulations, collective bargaining requirements, professional standards, and sound administrative practices. Participates in departmental planning related to occupancy, facilities, communications, summer operations, technology, emergency response, and other functions that intersect with the residential student experience. Additional Responsibilities: Serves on departmental, divisional, and University committees and work groups as assigned. Acts on behalf of the Executive Director of Residence Life and Chief Housing Officer in designated matters. Maintains awareness of contemporary research, legal developments, professional standards, and emerging practices in residential education and student affairs. Participates in relevant professional associations and represents Central Michigan University within the broader housing and residential education profession. Performs other duties as assigned. Supervision Exercised Supervisors are expected to support the Leadership Standards of Central Michigan University and foster a culture that inspires excellence. The Leadership Standards define how we lead and treat others on our team. As leaders, it is our responsibility to seek input from our teams to continuously improve our leadership abilities and our demonstration of the standards. Additionally, it is our responsibility to encourage and support the growth and development of those whom we lead. At CMU , we encourage employees to grow both personally and professionally, and thus advance CMU’s culture of excellence. Message to Applicants Central Michigan University is dedicated to fostering an environment that is reflective of the communities we serve. We are especially interested in highly qualified candidates who will advance and promote CMU’s mission, vision, and leadership standards. You must submit an on-line application in order to be considered as an applicant for this position. Cover letters may be addressed to the Hiring Committee. This position will remain open until filled. The university reserves the right to close the recruitment process once a sufficient applicant pool has been identified. For best consideration, please submit application materials by September 4, 2026 . Employee Group Professional & Administrative -Salary Staff Pay Level Pay Range $85,000 - $95,000 Division University Engagement and Student Affairs Department Residence Life Position Status Regular Position End Date Employment Status Full-Time FTE 1.0 Position Type 12 month Weekly Work Schedule Mon-Fri, 8:00 a.m. - 5:00 p.m. / weekends and evenings as needed Location Mount Pleasant, MI About the Department Residence Life consists of three units: Residential Education, Administration, and Facilities. Peak academic year occupancy is 4200 residents. CMU has a 1st and 2nd year occupancy requirement for undergraduate students. About CMU Central Michigan University has a more than 125-year legacy of preparing students to become leaders and changemakers in their communities and in their personal and professional lives. We serve nearly 15,500 students on our Mount Pleasant campus, in satellite locations around the state and throughout the country, and through flexible online programs. Many of our approximately 300 undergraduate, master’s, specialist and doctoral programs in the arts, media, business, education, human services, health professions, liberal arts, social sciences, medicine, science and engineering are nationally ranked for excellence. CMU leads the nation in leadership development programming through our Sarah R. Opperman Leadership Institute, and we are proud to be among only 5% of U.S. universities in the top two Carnegie research classifications. Our faculty work with graduate and undergraduate students in areas such as Great Lakes research, medical innovation, engineering technology and more. Central is home to 17 men’s and women’s Division 1 sports including football, basketball, gymnastics, baseball, wrestling and more. Our student-athletes achieve great success in competition and in the classroom, capturing Mid-American Conference championships and maintaining an average cumulative GPA of 3.17. CMU is located in Mount Pleasant, a community that blends the best of small-town living with big-city amenities. It’s part of the culturally varied and vibrant Great Lakes Bay Region that also includes Saginaw, Bay City, Midland and the state’s largest Native American community, centered on the Saginaw Chippewa Isabella Reservation in Mount Pleasant. Area residents enjoy the mix of outdoor activities, cultural events, shopping and dining options, and family attractions. Other major Michigan destinations and attractions — Lansing, Grand Rapids, Detroit, Traverse City, wineries, beaches, golf and ski resorts, and many more — are within easy reach of the city’s central location in Michigan’s Lower Peninsula. CMU employees enjoy access to a nationally recognized wellness program along with health care and benefits that exceed regional, state and national norms. CMU Leadership Standards Central Michigan University is a place where we value students and work for their success, where we act as family, and where employees are engaged, appreciated and have extraordinary opportunities to make a difference. We intentionally maintain and strengthen the hallmark CMU culture that sets us apart from our peers by expecting CMU leaders and employees to model the following Leadership Standards and develop them within their teams. Please review the Leadership Standards before applying for this position. EEO Statement CMU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and institution. CMU does not discriminate against persons based on age, color, disability, ethnicity, familial status, gender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, height, marital status, national origin, political persuasion, pregnancy, childbirth or related medical conditions, race, religion, sex, sex-based stereotypes, sexual orientation, transgender status, veteran status, or weight. If you wish to see “Know Your Rights ” posters, please click here . CMU does not discriminate on the basis of sex in the education program or activity that it operates, including admission and employment, and is required by Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972 not to discriminate in such a matter. Inquiries about the application of Title IX can be made to CMU’s Title IX Coordinator, the US Department of Education’s Assistant Secretary, or both. CMU’s Title IX Coordinator can be reached at: Phone: 989-774-3253 Office: 103 E. Preston St. Bovee University Center, suite 306 Mount Pleasant, MI 48858 Email: titleix@cmich.edu Posting Ends Open Until Filled Yes Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you first learn of this job opportunity?  Please list the specific advertising source or indicate if it was word of mouth. (Open Ended Question) * Are you currently an internal employee of CMU according to the university's definition? Yes No * If yes, to which employee group do you belong?  (If not applicable, select \"Not Applicable\"). Not Applicable Administrative Office Assistant Dispatcher Lieutenant Medical Faculty P&A, SO or Faculty Police Public Broadcasting Sergeant Service Maintenance Supervisory/Technical * Do you have at least a bachelor's degree in student affairs, higher education, or a related field? Yes No * Do you have at least five (5) years of progressively responsible experience in residence life, residential education, housing, student affairs, or a closely related field? Yes No * Do you have significant experience supervising, developing, and evaluating full-time professional staff, including employees who supervise others? Yes No * Supervisors are expected to support the Leadership Standards of Central Michigan University and foster a culture that inspires excellence. Please review the Leadership Standards before applying for this position and click “Yes” if you understand and support the culture that is important to CMU. Yes No Optional & Required Documents Required Documents Resume/Curriculum Vitae Cover Letter Optional Documents List of References Staff postings - Human Resources | Contact HR | (989)774-2010 | 109 Rowe Faculty postings - Faculty Personnel Services | Contact FPS | (989)774-3368 | 308 Warriner CMU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and institution.  CMU does not discriminate against persons based on age, color, disability, ethnicity, familial status, gender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, height, marital status, national origin, political persuasion, pregnancy, childbirth or related medical conditions, race, religion, sex, sex-based stereotypes, sexual orientation, transgender status, veteran status, or weight. Copyright © Central Michigan University, Mount Pleasant, Mich. 48859 | Phone 989-774-4000 All data submitted via this site is secure and encrypted using Secure Socket Layer (SSL) technology. SSL protects information as it crosses the Internet. To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
